  <details class="more">
    <summary><span class="chev"></span><span class="label-closed">Read the full description</span><span class="label-open">Hide description</span></summary>
    <div class="more-body">
      <p>Food Corporation of India depots hold paddy in stacks of roughly 2,772 gunny bags. Spoilage does not begin in the average, it begins in a pocket: a warm moist region forms, warm air carries moisture outward, moisture settles in cooler grain nearby, and the cycle reinforces itself. A bulk average hides all of that until the loss is visible.</p>
      <p>Eleven temperature and humidity sensors per bin report every six hours. I tessellated the sensor positions into tetrahedra with a Delaunay triangulation, converted temperature and relative humidity into grain moisture through an equilibrium isotherm, and integrated across the volume to produce a daily heat budget in megajoules and a daily water budget in kilograms, alongside interpolated field maps.</p>
      <p>Across June 2025, the Nalgonda bin lost heat on every day of the record. Miryalaguda showed a single moisture gain of 0.359 kg on 13 June that reversed the next day, with a near zero heat change on the same day, which reads as internal redistribution or a short condensation event rather than active spoilage. The field maps show a warm region fixed near sensor S2 and a cool region near S4 that barely moved all week. Pairing those maps with zone specific drying and turning, rather than drying whole stacks on a schedule, cut estimated paddy wastage across three depots from about 6.4% to about 4.5%.</p>
      <p>The method is deliberately honest about its own limits. A uniform half degree sensor error integrates to more energy than the daily signal itself, so the reported numbers are read as trends and signs rather than as precise quantities.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
